    "title": "LUNAR: LLM Unlearning via Neural Activation Redirection",
    "citation": "William F. Shen et al., 2025",
    "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07218",
    "authors": "William F. Shen; Xin-Chi Qiu; Meghdad Kurmanji; Alexandru Iacob; Lorenzo Sani; Yi-Hong Chen; Nicola Cancedda; N. Lane",
    "year": "2025",
    "original_source": false
  },
  "page_placements": [],
  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "LUNAR uses a difference-in-means activation vector and cites refusal steering as motivation, but uses its own activation-redirection method for factual unlearning rather than testing the claimed safety-refusal direction.",
    "gate_note": "The study does not ablate the harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction, add it to harmless requests, or test attacks that suppress it. Table 3 measures factual unlearning and knowledge-gap responses after redirecting activations toward harmful or unverifiable prompts; these are related to, but not the safety-refusal behavior in the claim.",
    "test_reason": "LUNAR borrows the difference-in-means steering idea and the harmful prompts dataset of the original. It computes a forget-set to reference-set vector and trains one MLP down-projection so that forgotten queries produce knowledge-gap responses. It reports only unlearning performance (forget/retain ROUGE1, control score, attacks) for its own method. It never ablates or adds the harmful-minus-harmless refusal direction, nor measures refusal of harmful requests.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that LUNAR, which redirects activations by updating a single down-projection, gives state-of-the-art, controllable and robust factual unlearning. They also argue that contrastive features are not needed for activation steering. They draw no conclusion on whether a single refusal direction mediates refusal.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "LUNAR computes a forget-versus-reference activation difference, then trains a selected MLP down-projection to redirect activations; it does not perform the original refusal-direction ablation or addition intervention.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reference set (harmful versus unverifiable prompts) changes reported control scores across several models; selected intervention layers also vary by model.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Redirecting forget-set activations toward the mean activation of harmful prompts or of unverifiable questions makes five 7-8B models answer forgotten factual queries with knowledge-gap or refusal-like text. Forget ROUGE1 is 0.000, retain ROUGE1 0.86-0.996 and control scores 0.62-0.71 (Table 3).",
        "proposed_claim": "A difference-in-means vector toward a refusal- or ignorance-eliciting prompt set can be baked into one MLP down-projection to make a model decline specific forgotten queries while keeping other knowledge.",
